note	First turbopfor rows: integer bit-packing, the fourth benchmark shape, measuring ONE source tree through three translation layers (plain C auto-vectorized by GCC, the SSE path via SIMDe 128-bit, the AVX2 path via SIMDe 256-bit onto a machine with no 256-bit datapath). The scalar row is the same source through the same compiler in all three configs and agrees to under 1% -- 1.668/1.655/1.659 and 1.779/1.787/1.780 cycles per integer -- which is the methodology control and should be read before any SIMD row. Two findings pulling opposite ways. bitn at 256 bits is 0.482 cycles/int against 0.666 at 128, 1.38x FASTER despite no 256-bit datapath, because a 256-bit op becomes a pair filling both ALU slots with the code unrolled by two: on a VLIW with spare slots the emulation buys ILP. But bitnd at 256 bits costs 104.220 cycles/int, 69x the 128-bit path and 58x scalar, because the AVX2 delta prefix-sum uses _mm256_permutevar8x32_epi32 and _mm256_permute2x128_si256 and LVX has no runtime-indexed permute at all. That independently confirms lvx-ssw inter-gather 14.2x for _mm_shuffle_epi8: two algorithms, two benchmarks, one missing instruction class, an lvx-mds question rather than a toolchain one. Practical rule: go wide for shift/mask/arithmetic, stay narrow for anything permuting. PROVENANCE: reads unreproducible-dirty-tree because lvx-csw lvx-gcc/gcc is dirty AT IMPORT -- the other session is working in it. The rows were produced by the installed lvx-mbr-gcc built from ddb027f279a, with no backend source newer than that binary when measured. Each row also carries cpu_model=minor in the TSV, so the model is recorded per row rather than left to gem5 default atomic.
